{
  "correction_rules": [
    "Uptrend underprediction (horizon > ~15d): when the recent 30d history shows a clear down-then-up turn (local trough followed by higher highs), the model systematically underestimates the subsequent rally; apply an upward drift add-on that increases with horizon (small in days 1–7, larger in days 15–30).",
    "Late-horizon level bias: forecasts for days ~20–30 are too low by several €/t in rising regimes; use a horizon-dependent bias correction (e.g., +0 to +1 early, ramping to +3 to +7 by day 30 when momentum is positive).",
    "Lag/slow reaction to acceleration: the model captures direction early but misses the speed of the move (true series steepens while forecast stays flat); if the last ~5–10 realized days show accelerating gains, steepen the forecast slope (increase daily increments) rather than shifting the whole path uniformly.",
    "Post-breakout underestimation: after prices break above the recent 30d range (new local highs), the model mean-reverts too aggressively; reduce mean-reversion strength and allow persistence (keep forecasts closer to the breakout level, especially days 10–30).",
    "Volatility compression: forecast paths are smoother than realized outcomes, missing late spikes; widen the forecast distribution / add upside convexity in bullish regimes (e.g., add a positive skew adjustment rather than symmetric noise)."
  ]
}
